From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0490: dual-write resolve: a direct resolve also writes the resolved texture that covers its destination, so that texture is not loaded again (Direct3D 12, resolve_dual_write)

Source: Ours, written for this SDK (O1; lever 1 of $WORK/scratch/R3/REPORT.md). It builds on the SDK's direct resolve
  (resolve_direct.xesli, Renan Queiroz's direct-resolve patch) and on texture_load_direct_unscaled (his texture-load-direct
  patch, whose load shaders define what a resolved texture holds); the shader sources they include are Xenia's
  (BSD-3-Clause). Not run in the game: built (SDK build of the series with this patch) and run on a WARP adapter
  (the harness below), nothing more
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: Each frame the resolved surfaces of a 1080p frame (about 70 MB of guest data) are written to the shared memory by a
  resolve and loaded as textures straight afterwards (texture_load_direct_unscaled: one read of that memory and one write
  of the texture, two barriers and a dispatch each; R3 puts that at 1.6-2.2 ms of GPU time on the Radeon 880M [INF]).
  Cvar resolve_dual_write (bool, hot reload, default false until a run has validated it): a direct resolve that covers a
  texture the game sampled at its destination before (a resolved_unscaled texture of the texture cache: tiled, 2D, one
  level, 32 or 64 bits a texel, loaded straight in) also writes that texture from the resolve shader, and the texture is
  marked up to date, so its load is skipped; the shared memory is written exactly as before (the rain's vertex fetches,
  the vinyl readback and every other reader of guest memory see the same bytes). What the shader stores is what the load
  shader of the texture makes of the dwords the resolve has stored for the texel (the load's endian swap over the
  resolve's, then the load's depth transformation), so it is the same whatever the two endianness settings are: the
  picture is exact by construction. Expected gain 0.8-1.1 ms at 1080p [INF]: the loads' reads, dispatches and barriers go,
  their writes move into the resolve. Cvar resolve_dual_write_verify (int, hot reload, default 0): every Nth texture a
  resolve writes is also loaded the usual way into a texture of its own and both are copied back and compared. Direct3D 12
  only; Vulkan is untouched.
  Log, every 10 s while resolve_dual_write is on (counts since the previous line; the second line only if it has
  something to say):
    [resolve-dual-write] N resolves dual-written, M loads skipped, K fallbacks (<reason> <count>, ...)[, W dual writes unused (...)]
    [resolve-dual-write] verify: V textures checked, B differ (X bytes), S not checked (too many checks in flight)
  Reasons (first 11 of ResolveDualWriteFallback): scaled, no-direct, stretch, msaa-source, shader, destination,
  no-texture, layout, partial, not-uav, pipeline. And once per pipeline `D3D12RenderTargetCache: the dual-write direct
  <name> pipeline is made`. With resolve_dual_write_verify, per check that finds a difference
  `[resolve-dual-write] verify WxH at 0xADDR: MISMATCH, N of M bytes differ, first at texel (x, y): ...` (an ERROR) and, for
  the first 8 that match, `... : 0 of M bytes differ`.
Bucket: REDO

0490-dual-write-resolve.patch (mcla-x86 O1; lever 1 of $WORK/scratch/R3/REPORT.md)

Change.
  * src/graphics/shaders/resolve_direct.xesli: XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_DUAL_WRITE (HLSL only; every addition is under it, so none of
    the existing 56 resolve_direct_* shaders changed: build_resolve_shaders.py --check says "same" for all of them). It adds
    two root constants after the source ones (xe_resolve_dual_info: the texture's xenos::Endian in bits 0:1 and the
    transformation in bits 2:3; xe_resolve_dual_size: its width and height; the stretch pair keeps its place as two unused
    constants, because the host's DirectResolveSourceConstants always has it), a typed UAV at u1 (RWTexture2DArray<uint>,
    or <uint4> with XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_DUAL_WRITE_64BPP), XeResolveDualGetInfo, and XeResolveDualStore4Texels32bpb /
    XeResolveDualStore2Texels64bpb: the dwords the resolve stores for 4 (32 bits) or 2 (64 bits) consecutive texels are
    endian-swapped as texture_load_direct_scaled.xesli does, transformed (none, XeFloat20e4To32 of the 24 high bits for 24_8
    FLOAT depth, XeUNorm24To32 for 24_8 depth: the XE_TEXTURE_LOAD_32BPB_TRANSFORMs of the depth direct loads) and stored
    at the texel (x, y) = the resolve pixel + dest_xy_offset_scaled, which is the texel the address of the resolve's store is
    the tiled address of; stores outside the texture's width and height are skipped.
  * src/graphics/shaders/resolve_direct_{fast,full}_{32,64}bpp.xesli: the stores after the existing ones, under the define.
    The 64 bpp ones define XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_DUAL_WRITE_64BPP.
  * src/graphics/shaders/resolve_direct_batched_{fast,full}_{32,64}bpp_dual.cs.xesl (4 new: XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_BATCHED_LOADS +
    XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_DUAL_WRITE) and src/graphics/shaders/bytecode/d3d12_5_1/resolve_direct_batched_*_dual_cs.h (4 new
    DXBC: fast32 38,784 B, fast64 9,776 B, full32 60,816 B, full64 58,776 B). Only the batched, single-sampled-source,
    unscaled variants; the other kinds of resolve (multisampled source, the FSR stretch, resolution scaling, 8/16/128 bits a
    pixel) are counted as fallbacks and go the usual way. The dual pipelines are used whatever resolve_direct_batched_loads
    says (the values are the same either way).
  * src/graphics/pipeline/texture/cache.cpp, include/rex/graphics/pipeline/texture/cache.h: an index of the cache's textures
    that have resolved_unscaled by base page (Texture's constructor adds, its destructor removes; only the cache's own
    textures, the ones in its usage list), GetResolvedTexturesAtBasePage, and MarkRangeAsResolvedWithTextureWritten (the
    usual MarkRangeAsResolved and the written texture's MakeUpToDateAndWatch inside one critical section, so a write by the CPU
    can't come in between and leave the texture newer than the memory).
  * src/graphics/d3d12/texture_cache.cpp, include/rex/graphics/d3d12/texture_cache.h: the two cvars; FindResolveDualWriteTarget
    (the texture at the destination base: tiled, 2D, one level, no mips data, not scaled, not signed-separate, the resolve's
    pitch, the resolve's bytes a texel, loadable straight in (direct_load_uav_format and a direct load shader of the same
    size), and covered by the resolve (width and height not above the resolve area; the area starts at (0, 0) because the
    caller requires zero offsets); the most recently used if several; why not otherwise), BeginResolveDualWrite (the UAV
    written as texture_load_direct writes it, the texture to the UAV state with the barrier texture_load_direct pushes),
    FinishResolveDualWrite (the memory range marked resolved, the texture up to date, the count, the verify), the counters,
    their 10 s line, and the verify (a texture of its own loaded by LoadTextureDataFromResidentMemoryImpl, both copied to a
    readback buffer, compared when the submission completes in CompletedSubmissionUpdated, at most 4 in flight). A texture
    a resolve has written is flagged until a draw (RequestTextures) or the presentation (RequestSwapTexture) uses it
    (loads skipped) or a load or another dual write comes first (unused).
  * src/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp, include/rex/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.h: kResolveDirectDualShaders, a
    root signature with the direct resolve's four parameters and a fifth (the UAV table at u1) and 11 constants, the
    pipelines made on first use (a failure is remembered), PrepareDirectResolveDualWrite (single-sampled direct source, not
    stretching, not scaled; the destination: no array, no offset, at a page; then the texture), and Resolve: one more one-use
    descriptor, the dual root signature, the texture's UAV and constants, FinishResolveDualWrite in place of
    MarkRangeAsResolved.

Why it is exact. The load of the texture reads what the resolve stored: for the texel (x, y) the dwords at the tiled address of
(x, y) with the resolve's pitch from the destination base, which is where the resolve stored the pixel (x, y) because the
caller requires the texture's base to be the resolve's destination base, its pitch to be the resolve's, no offset of the area
from the base and the same number of bytes a texel (tiling depends on nothing else). The texture is the whole of its layout
that the load would read (width x height texels), all covered by the resolve, so no texel keeps what an earlier load or
write left there. Every other texture over the range is invalidated by MarkRangeAsResolved as before; a later write by
the CPU or the GPU invalidates this one too (it is watched again).

How the bytecode is made (prebuilt, checked in, never compiled by the SDK's build): as in 0460, Renan's
launcher/dev/build_resolve_shaders.py from a xenia-project/xenia tree at 0b2ffa31485318f18838afa142cdb51cf1b35213 (sparse
checkout of src/xenia/gpu/shaders and src/xenia/ui/shaders), fxc 10.0.26100.0 cs_5_1 /O3 /Qstrip_*:
    VULKAN_SDK=<any dir> python -I build_resolve_shaders.py --sdk <SDK tree> --xenia <xenia>/src/xenia --api d3d12 <names>
with the names resolve_direct_batched_{fast,full}_{32,64}bpp_dual_cs. `--api d3d12 --check` over all 60 resolve_direct_*
shaders of the tree says "same" (the 56 that existed and the 4 new).

Checks: (1) clang++ 20.1.8 -fsyntax-only of src/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp, src/graphics/d3d12/texture_cache.cpp
and src/graphics/pipeline/texture/cache.cpp with the SDK's Release flags: 0 errors, no new warnings. (2) `python
tools/prepare_sdk.py --work $WORK/scratch/O1 build` of series 88 plus this patch: 859 build steps, installed, no error, no
warning in the new code. (3) The disassembly of resolve_direct_batched_fast_32bpp_dual (fxc /Fc) has the cbuffer layout the
host writes (11 constants, the dual pair at dwords 9 and 10), U0 the shared memory typed buffer, U1 the typed texture2darray,
8 typed stores to U1. (4) A throwaway D3D12 harness on the WARP adapter ($WORK/scratch/O1/h/harness.cpp, built with the
toolchain's clang++ -ld3d12 -ldxgi) that creates the dual root signature of this patch (11 constants, u0 shared memory,
t0/t1 sources, u1 texture) and the texture load root signature of D3D12TextureCache::Initialize, resolves random 128x64
render target data with the four dual shaders (the 1x source views as D3D12RenderTargetCache::Resolve binds them: 8_8_8_8
float view; 16_16_16_16 as the uint view) into a shared memory buffer and the texture, then loads a second texture from
that memory with texture_load_{32bpb,64bpb,depth_float,depth_unorm}_direct_scaled (the SDK's DXBC, constants as
LoadTextureDataFromResidentMemoryImpl sets them for an unscaled direct load) and compares them: every resolve endianness
(0-3, and 8in64 for 64 bits) against every texture endianness (0-3), the three transformations for 32 bits, textures of 128x64,
100x50 and 7x8 texels (the bounds checks), fast and full shaders: 408 cases, 1,801,728 texels, 0 differing bytes; the
negative control (the dual write told texture endianness 0 while the load uses 2) differs in 32,620 bytes, so the compare
can fail. It does not test the host code (texture lookup, state tracking, watches, barriers), only the shaders and the
constants layout.
Verify (VER): run n1080:B with --resolve_dual_write=true --resolve_dual_write_verify=7 for a minute or two after the warm-up
and look for `[resolve-dual-write] verify ... MISMATCH` (there must be none) and for the verify counts in the 10 s lines (V
above 0, B equal to 0); then the same without verify against --resolve_dual_write=false for the frame time and for a
frozen-frame pixel comparison (the pictures must be identical). The first line of each run should show dual writes about
as many as the resolved textures a frame samples (R3: about 45), loads skipped close to that, and fallbacks mostly `no-texture`
for the first frame only.

